WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:06.799
Okay, let's continue exploring server-client setup as we look at working with ShotGrid.

2
00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:11.789
Now, before we get too far into where ShotGrid fits among the three pillars of our collaborative

3
00:00:11.789 --> 00:00:17.109
production pipeline and workflows, I want to talk about ShotGrid as a possible source

4
00:00:17.109 --> 00:00:18.469
control solution.

5
00:00:18.469 --> 00:00:25.820
So, ShotGrid, formerly Shotgun, is a product from Autodesk that can also work as a version

6
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.480
control solution in place of Perforce.

7
00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:34.280
ShotGrid tool integration is available in Unreal Engine, but only when the ShotGrid plugin

8
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.579
is enabled.

9
00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:39.920
ShotGrid Toolkit configuration is project-centric.

10
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.340
It's set up much the same way a Maya project file is organized and then stored on a server

11
00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.350
where it can be accessed from that storage location.

12
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:54.270
This pipeline maintains that same Maya-type project structure and then uses ShotGrid

13
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:59.750
as a project management system in order to maintain and manage those assets inside that

14
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.149
single Toolkit project configuration.

15
00:01:03.149 --> 00:01:07.590
While this is a viable alternative solution for version control, there are some other

16
00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:14.329
considerations that must be made before choosing ShotGrid as your version control solution.

17
00:01:14.329 --> 00:01:20.730
First up, ShotGrid Toolkit is a pipeline toolkit based on the ShotGrid platform.

18
00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:25.590
It does make this easier to install or write custom tools for studio integration, and

19
00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:32.319
Epic Games does implement that feature function as part of how we utilize ShotGrid.

20
00:01:32.319 --> 00:01:34.540
Toolkit is a file-based system.

21
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:39.219
It does help you organize where things are stored on disk so that everything is nicely

22
00:01:39.219 --> 00:01:45.840
structured based on that familiar Maya project structure, which is a structure that is probably

23
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.760
very familiar for most of your creative team members.

24
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.000
Toolkit is an assistant.

25
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.760
It does not try to take over or abstract the data in your pipeline, but instead it

26
00:01:56.799 --> 00:02:00.840
is there to provide artists with tools that are designed to make finding your information

27
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:07.769
easier for your team, and hopefully that'll help avoid making mistakes during production.

28
00:02:07.769 --> 00:02:12.210
Toolkit is mostly used to help you share your work by storing all of its publishes

29
00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:18.819
in ShotGrid, making it easy to share updates and work as it moves through the production.

30
00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:23.939
One thing to be aware of is that ShotGrid as version control has an inherent danger

31
00:02:23.939 --> 00:02:27.520
when Toolkit is used for this purpose.

32
00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:33.020
ShotGrid as version control can break fairly easily if you aren't careful.

33
00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:38.180
Any artist can gain direct access to the main project file storage location if they

34
00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.919
know the file path.

35
00:02:40.919 --> 00:02:45.360
Directly accessing the project files instead of going through the proper steps of accessing

36
00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:51.479
using ShotGrid tool integrations in DCC applications will overwrite that stored information

37
00:02:51.479 --> 00:02:54.400
and will potentially break the file path.

38
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.479
This is not true version control, as it's left to the artist to ensure that the files

39
00:02:58.479 --> 00:03:00.780
are versioned when saved.

40
00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:07.199
Overall, ShotGrid is not as robust a version control solution as Perforce, but it does

41
00:03:07.199 --> 00:03:10.139
have other important uses.

42
00:03:10.139 --> 00:03:14.159
If we stick with Perforce as our source control solution, the next step in our production

43
00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:17.250
workflow is going to be production management.

44
00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.889
Since we've been talking about ShotGrid, that is actually what ShotGrid's purpose

45
00:03:20.889 --> 00:03:26.030
will be in our collaborative production pipeline, and that's how Epic Games utilizes ShotGrid

46
00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:27.740
in production.

47
00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:32.259
As artists build assets, they can submit them to the Perforce Depot for other artists

48
00:03:32.259 --> 00:03:36.219
in different departments to work on in real time.

49
00:03:36.219 --> 00:03:39.580
ShotGrid is how Epic Games handles our production management for asset tracking and

50
00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:45.180
assignment using a media importer to add turntable renders, screenshots, color guides,

51
00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.860
or any completed step in the asset creation process.

52
00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:54.620
Artists can upload the asset media to ShotGrid, and Epic Games uses a customized LaunchBox

53
00:03:54.620 --> 00:04:00.830
Python tool that we created expressly for the submission of dailies and media assets.

54
00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:05.110
After the creative team members upload the asset media to ShotGrid, the production management

55
00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:11.189
team then maintains organization, reviews work, leaves or manages notations, and begins

56
00:04:11.189 --> 00:04:16.230
the next steps in our collaborative review process.

57
00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:20.970
ShotGrid for production management allows for project planning, asset tracking, media

58
00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.560
management, and more.

59
00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:28.459
At Epic Games, we use ShotGrid to manage our own internal projects on Fortnite, Fortnite

60
00:04:28.459 --> 00:04:33.009
Cinematics, and other related games in the Fortnite Metaverse.

61
00:04:33.009 --> 00:04:37.649
One thing to note is that the UCE project that you see pictured here in this slide

62
00:04:37.649 --> 00:04:42.610
to the right is still in the early stages of development and will soon be updated

63
00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:46.610
to match the Epic Games production format.

64
00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:47.870
That's all for this section.

65
00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.649
In our next section, we will explore version control in Unreal Engine.
